test_rsrestart — server crash + RS restart resilience test
=============================================================

Phase 0c chunk 8: pins the contract that RS reincarnates a dead
boot-module server transparently to its clients. The cached `ds`
port id must keep working across the restart because RS calls
`port_transfer` to assign the new ds thread the same port id
before it runs.

We use the `ds` server because it's the lowest-cross-section
restart target: pure key/value store, present on both x86 and
aarch64 (unlike `nic`), no dependents in the boot path, no
device grants, no client code in the rest of the test
fixture that would race the restart.

The trigger is a debug `DS_CMD_PANIC_SELF` (5) command added to
`oskit/servers/ds.lsysl` for this test alone — the handler
replies status=0 so the caller unblocks, then `exit()`s. The
kernel terminates the process; PM's `pm_handle_exit` notices the
parent is RS and calls `notify_send_to(rs_tid, dead_pid)`; RS's
`rs_monitor` loop wakes, calls `rs_handle_crash(5)`, which:

  1. reloads the ds boot module into a fresh process,
  2. calls `port_transfer(old_port, new_main_tid)` so the new
     ds thread inherits the same port id,
  3. re-applies the ds privilege + ipc-send mask,
  4. blocks in `rs_wait_ready()` until the new ds calls `rs_ready()`.

While RS is in the middle of (1)–(4), the test's next
`ipc_send` to the cached port id either delivers immediately
(if the new ds is already past `port_create` + `ipc_recv`) or
queues in the port FIFO and unblocks when the new ds runs. We
don't poll explicitly; the kernel's send-then-block path handles
the wait.

Test plan
---------

  1. `port_lookup("ds")`. Expect a non-negative id.
  2. Round-trip a publish + retrieve to confirm ds works
     pre-panic. Sentinel value 42.
  3. Send `DS_CMD_PANIC_SELF`. Expect ipc_send returns 0 and
     `reply[0] == 0`.
  4. Round-trip a *different* sentinel (99) post-panic via the
     same cached port. Both publish and retrieve must succeed.
     A different value rules out the test reading a stale reply
     from a still-alive old ds.
  5. Print `rsrestart: ok`.

Pass: prints `rsrestart: ok`. Failure prints `rsrestart: bad N`
where N tags the failing step. A hang (no output) means RS
stopped restarting servers — the bug class this chunk pins.

    module oskit.bin.test_rsrestart

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, port_lookup, ipc_send}


    val DS_CMD_PUBLISH    = 1
    val DS_CMD_RETRIEVE   = 2
    val DS_CMD_PANIC_SELF = 5
    val DS_TYPE_INT       = 1


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("rsrestart: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


Wire helpers — duplicate of the ds client library (which we don't
import, to keep the test self-contained and avoid coupling the
restart test to client API churn).

    ds_publish_int(port: int, key: string, value: int) -> int
        val klen = len(key)
        var msg: [80]byte
        msg[0] = byte(DS_CMD_PUBLISH)
        msg[1] = byte(DS_TYPE_INT)
        msg[2] = byte(klen)
        val kp: *byte = *byte(key)
        var i = 0
        while i < klen
            msg[3 + i] = kp[i]
            i += 1
        msg[3 + klen]     = byte((value >> 24) & 0xFF)
        msg[3 + klen + 1] = byte((value >> 16) & 0xFF)
        msg[3 + klen + 2] = byte((value >> 8) & 0xFF)
        msg[3 + klen + 3] = byte(value & 0xFF)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3 + klen + 4, reply, 1)
        if r != 0
            return -1
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -1
        0


    ds_retrieve_int(port: int, key: string) -> int
        val klen = len(key)
        var msg: [48]byte
        msg[0] = byte(DS_CMD_RETRIEVE)
        msg[1] = byte(klen)
        val kp: *byte = *byte(key)
        var i = 0
        while i < klen
            msg[2 + i] = kp[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [68]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2 + klen, reply, 68)
        if r != 0
            return -2147483647
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -2147483647
        if int(reply[1]) != DS_TYPE_INT
            return -2147483647
        val b0 = int(reply[3]) & 0xFF
        val b1 = int(reply[4]) & 0xFF
        val b2 = int(reply[5]) & 0xFF
        val b3 = int(reply[6]) & 0xFF
        (b0 << 24) | (b1 << 16) | (b2 << 8) | b3


    main(argv: []string)
        val port = port_lookup("ds")
        if port < 0
            fail(1)
            return

Pre-panic round trip. Confirms the ds we're about to kill is up
and answering normally before we exercise the restart path.

        if ds_publish_int(port, "rstest", 42) != 0
            fail(2)
            return
        if ds_retrieve_int(port, "rstest") != 42
            fail(3)
            return

Send the panic command. The handler replies before exiting, so
this ipc_send returns 0. After the reply lands, ds exits → PM
notifies RS → RS reincarnates ds.

        var pmsg: [1]byte
        pmsg[0] = byte(DS_CMD_PANIC_SELF)
        var preply: [1]byte
        val pr = ipc_send(port, pmsg, 1, preply, 1)
        if pr != 0
            fail(4)
            return
        if preply[0] != 0
            fail(5)
            return

Post-panic round trip on the *same* cached port id. The send
either reaches the live new ds, or queues in the port FIFO until
the new ds runs `port_create` + `ipc_recv`. Either way it
eventually completes. Sentinel 99 rules out reading a leftover
reply from the dead instance.

        if ds_publish_int(port, "rstest", 99) != 0
            fail(6)
            return
        if ds_retrieve_int(port, "rstest") != 99
            fail(7)
            return

        puts("rsrestart: ok\n")
        exit()
